Let me think about this differently. Maybe the issue is not in `_gnutls_x509_read_string` but in `_decode_pkcs12_auth_safe` itself. Let me look at the function again:

```c
result = _gnutls_x509_read_string(pkcs12, "authSafe.content", &auth_safe, ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING, 1);
if (result < 0) {
    gnutls_assert();
    goto cleanup;
}
```

If `_gnutls_x509_read_string` succeeds, `auth_safe.data` is allocated and `auth_safe.size` is set. Then:

```c
result = asn1_der_decoding(&c2, auth_safe.data, auth_safe.size, error_str);
```

The `auth_safe.data` is passed to `asn1_der_decoding`. If `auth_safe.data` contains uninitialized bytes (because `gnutls_malloc` doesn't zero-initialize), then `asn1_der_decoding` might read uninitialized memory.

But wait - `asn1_read_value` should write exactly `auth_safe.size` bytes into `auth_safe.data`. So all bytes up to `auth_safe.size` should be initialized.

Unless... `asn1_read_value` writes fewer bytes than `auth_safe.size`. Let me check:

In `x509_read_value`:
```c
result = asn1_read_value_type(c, root, NULL, &len, &etype);
...
tmp = gnutls_malloc((size_t) len + 1);
...
if (len > 0) {
    result = asn1_read_value(c, root, tmp, &len);
    ...
    ret->size = (unsigned) len;
}
```

The initial `len` is the buffer size needed. `asn1_read_value` writes the data and updates `len` to the actual size. So `ret->size` should be the actual data size, and all bytes up to `ret->size` should be initialized.

